From: android-kitkat-tls12 <android-kitkat-tls12@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 25/30] Tests - SSLv3 and RC4 stay usable when an app enables
 them explicitly

Layer A changes only the defaults: SSLv3 and RC4 suites leave
the default protocols and cipher suites but stay supported. No test so
far completed a handshake with explicitly enabled SSLv3 (the SCSV test
test_SSLSocket_sendsTlsFallbackScsv_InappropriateFallback_Failure
enables SSLv3 but expects the handshake to fail), and RC4 was covered
only as one suite among all in getSupportedCipherSuites_connect.

New SSLSocketTest cases on the default AndroidOpenSSL test context:
- test_SSLSocket_legacyOptIn_SSLv3: SSLv3 enabled on both ends
  negotiates SSLv3; a client with the default protocols fails against
  an SSLv3-only server.
- test_SSLSocket_legacyOptIn_RC4: SSL_RSA_WITH_RC4_128_SHA enabled on
  both ends negotiates that suite over TLSv1.2; a client with the
  default suites fails against an RC4-only server.

Tests only; no product change.

Android 4.3 port (android-4.3_r3) of patch 0026 of the 4.4 series
(renumbered: the 4.4 patch 0025, the ssl.SocketFactory.provider hook of
the TLS 1.3 layer, is not part of this series). The same change; only
line offsets differ.
---
 .../libcore/javax/net/ssl/SSLSocketTest.java  | 94 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 94 insertions(+)

diff --git a/luni/src/test/java/libcore/javax/net/ssl/SSLSocketTest.java b/luni/src/test/java/libcore/javax/net/ssl/SSLSocketTest.java
index 0d6d5f662..c5e0cbb24 100644
--- a/luni/src/test/java/libcore/javax/net/ssl/SSLSocketTest.java
+++ b/luni/src/test/java/libcore/javax/net/ssl/SSLSocketTest.java
@@ -2022,6 +2022,100 @@ public class SSLSocketTest extends TestCase {
         }
     }
 
+    /**
+     * kktls (layer A changes only the defaults): SSLv3 is off by default, but an app that enables it
+     * explicitly on both ends still completes an SSLv3 handshake, and a default client still
+     * refuses an SSLv3-only server.
+     */
+    public void test_SSLSocket_legacyOptIn_SSLv3() throws Exception {
+        String[] sslv3 = new String[] { "SSLv3" };
+        SSLSession session = legacyOptInHandshake(sslv3, null, sslv3, null);
+        assertEquals("SSLv3", session.getProtocol());
+        try {
+            legacyOptInHandshake(null, null, sslv3, null);
+            fail("a client with the default protocols negotiated with an SSLv3-only server");
+        } catch (SSLException expected) {
+        }
+    }
+
+    /**
+     * kktls (layer A changes only the defaults): RC4 is not a default suite, but an app that enables
+     * an RC4 suite explicitly on both ends still completes the handshake with it (TLSv1.2 with the
+     * default protocols), and a default client still refuses an RC4-only server.
+     */
+    public void test_SSLSocket_legacyOptIn_RC4() throws Exception {
+        String[] rc4 = new String[] { "SSL_RSA_WITH_RC4_128_SHA" };
+        SSLSession session = legacyOptInHandshake(null, rc4, null, rc4);
+        assertEquals("SSL_RSA_WITH_RC4_128_SHA", session.getCipherSuite());
+        assertEquals("TLSv1.2", session.getProtocol());
+        try {
+            legacyOptInHandshake(null, null, null, rc4);
+            fail("a client with the default cipher suites negotiated with an RC4-only server");
+        } catch (SSLException expected) {
+        }
+    }
+
+    /**
+     * Connects a client socket of the default (AndroidOpenSSL) test context to its server socket.
+     * A null array keeps that socket's defaults. Returns the client's session after a completed
+     * handshake on both ends; if the client's handshake fails, throws the client's exception.
+     */
+    private static SSLSession legacyOptInHandshake(final String[] clientProtocols,
+                                                   final String[] clientCipherSuites,
+                                                   final String[] serverProtocols,
+                                                   final String[] serverCipherSuites)
+            throws Exception {
+        TestSSLContext context = TestSSLContext.create();
+        final SSLSocket client = (SSLSocket)
+            context.clientContext.getSocketFactory().createSocket(context.host, context.port);
+        final SSLSocket server = (SSLSocket) context.serverSocket.accept();
+        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);
+        try {
+            Future<Void> s = executor.submit(new Callable<Void>() {
+                    public Void call() throws Exception {
+                        if (serverProtocols != null) {
+                            server.setEnabledProtocols(serverProtocols);
+                        }
+                        if (serverCipherSuites != null) {
+                            server.setEnabledCipherSuites(serverCipherSuites);
+                        }
+                        server.startHandshake();
+                        return null;
+                    }
+                });
+            Future<SSLSession> c = executor.submit(new Callable<SSLSession>() {
+                    public SSLSession call() throws Exception {
+                        if (clientProtocols != null) {
+                            client.setEnabledProtocols(clientProtocols);
+                        }
+                        if (clientCipherSuites != null) {
+                            client.setEnabledCipherSuites(clientCipherSuites);
+                        }
+                        client.startHandshake();
+                        return client.getSession();
+                    }
+                });
+            executor.shutdown();
+            SSLSession session;
+            try {
+                session = c.get(30, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
+            } catch (ExecutionException e) {
+                // the server's handshake fails too; closing the client below ends it
+                if (e.getCause() instanceof Exception) {
+                    throw (Exception) e.getCause();
+                }
+                throw e;
+            }
+            s.get(30, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
+            return session;
+        } finally {
+            client.close();
+            server.close();
+            context.close();
+            executor.shutdownNow();
+        }
+    }
+
     public static void main (String[] args) {
         new SSLSocketTest().stress_test_TestSSLSocketPair_create();
     }
